A||||5||owenslee||Cast Iron 2.8 Bow Tie 4 bolt  Long Block ??||||5||
Z||000000||owenslee||03-24-2001||12:51 AM||||To all the 60 deg v-6 experts...<P>A friend just closed his engine building shop and had  a CAST IRON Bow Tie Short Block in storage for the 2.8 that I cannot identify in any of my Chevrolet Parts Catalogs.<P>I am told this may be an early very limited production racing block.  I'm inclined to believe it may be  the predecessor to the aluminum 2.8 Bow Tie.<P>The block has steel splayed 4 bolt mains with the Bow Tie emblem cast into the side of the block. The casting number is 10044985. The number 985R is also cast.<P>This particular block has 3.560 Bore with Wiseco Forged flattop pistons with valve reliefs machined for the splayed valve aluminum head. <P>The rods are Carillo mounted to a production  2.99 stroke crank that has the timing slot center counterweight.<P>The cam is a steel billet roller having .620 lift. The block is cut for roller rocker retainers.<P>The owner claims the short block was originally built by Kaetech for an alcohol burning application.<P>The engine has been run, but it appears to have only been dyno time as the bores appear new. The piston domes MAY have a thermal coating as the finish appears smooth but porous. The appearance is  not your typical machined aluminum finish.<P>The harmonic balancer has been machined into a ring that has an additional reluctor mounted on an adjacent bracket and the ring is degreed.  <P>Although the displacement is only 178.5 cu. in. this block would appear to be a possible candidate for turbocharging with a cam change and some piston modifications to lower compression.<P>In any event, can anyone I.D. this block or offer any information?<P>I am very curious...<P>The short block is for sale but no price is yet set.<P>BTW, the same owner is a relative of Kenny Schraeder  and has a 4.3 V-6 Bow Tie Block with a forged odd fire  Windberg knife edge crank, Bow Tie rods and J.E. Pistons that came from Schraeder that is also for sale. The parts are apparently  "left overs" after the switch  from V-6s back to the V-8s in Nascar. The block is 4.125 finished bore and new. The crank is a 3.75 stroke and is .010/.010 ( 5.0L /301 cu. in. V-6 Fiero?)<P>Kerry||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shaun41178||03-24-2001||11:55 AM||||keep me p[osted how much he wants for it.  I would like to see it and or have it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||03-24-2001||04:12 PM||||I got my engine out of a Cavalier Z24. Its a 3.1, with a 4 bolt block, cast iron heads. I was told this was a special order item from dealers and not in the production cars. I can turn 7000 rpm in it with a turbo, no problems yet and I do run it hard (knock on wood, this is its 3rd year in my car)||||reg||1
